WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.220
A newborn mouse that lacks the muscle kinase MuSK cannot breathe.

2
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.700
Its motor axons reach the diaphragm, but no neuromuscular junction forms.

3
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.920
A synapse is not built whenever an axon touches a target.

4
00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:21.760
A healthy junction packs its acetylcholine receptors under the nerve ending.

5
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.420
Where do the receptors go, and who tells them?

6
00:00:25.183 --> 00:00:26.803
The muscle can start the job alone.

7
00:00:27.423 --> 00:00:31.843
In mouse embryos with no motor neurons, the diaphragm still builds a band of

8
00:00:31.843 --> 00:00:33.483
receptor clusters across its middle.

9
00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:35.803
The muscle laid down a pre -pattern.

10
00:00:36.323 --> 00:00:39.743
That pre -pattern needs MuSK, but it does not need agrin.

11
00:00:40.403 --> 00:00:44.603
When the nerve does arrive, the band narrows to the clusters beneath the terminal.

12
00:00:45.263 --> 00:00:49.203
Clusters can form without a nerve, but a nerve is still needed for a synapse.

13
00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:51.813
The nerve adds its own signal.

14
00:00:52.293 --> 00:00:56.933
It makes a protein called agrin and sends it into the basal lamina, the thin layer

15
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:58.593
around each muscle fibre.

16
00:00:59.293 --> 00:01:02.173
Agrin binds Lrp4 on the muscle surface.

17
00:01:02.713 --> 00:01:08.473
Lrp4 forms a complex with MuSK, and Dok7 inside the muscle boosts MuSK.

18
00:01:08.473 --> 00:01:12.353
Downstream of musc, wraps and holds the receptors together.

19
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:14.167
Here is the puzzle.

20
00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.827
The muscle can cluster receptors with no agrin at all.

21
00:01:18.247 --> 00:01:21.627
Yet mice that lack agrin lose most of their clusters.

22
00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:23.947
The nerve brings a second signal.

23
00:01:24.627 --> 00:01:29.627
Acetylcholine, released at every terminal, disperses receptor clusters that agrin

24
00:01:29.627 --> 00:01:30.627
does not protect.

25
00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.810
Test it with mutant mice.

26
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:38.530
Remove agrin, and acetylcholine disperses the unprotected clusters, so most are

27
00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.990
lost. Remove the enzyme that makes acetylcholine, and the main disperser is gone.

28
00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.450
The muscle keeps more clusters, over a wider band.

29
00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:53.010
Now remove both. Clusters return, built by the muscle's own MuSK program.

30
00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:55.823
The double mutant is the telling case.

31
00:01:56.223 --> 00:02:00.663
If agrin were required to start clusters, losing acetylcholine could not bring them

32
00:02:00.663 --> 00:02:06.423
back. So in the animal, a major job of agrin is to protect clusters from dispersal

33
00:02:06.423 --> 00:02:11.583
by acetylcholine. In developing mouse muscle, clustering is a competition between

34
00:02:11.583 --> 00:02:14.723
two forces. Agrin and MuSK facilitate.

35
00:02:15.503 --> 00:02:16.543
Acetylcholine inhibits.

36
00:02:16.543 --> 00:02:21.443
Keep this rule: the muscle starts clusters, and agrin protects them.
